<p>The Emerson Institute was Mandan's second public building, following the log-cabin county courthouse. It originated with “a debating society which was conducted for the mental recreation and improvement of the first settlers of the town.” The group approached the {% place "northern-pacific-railway" %}Northern Pacific Railway{% endplace %}, which offered three lots for “reasonable consideration” on the condition that they be used for a lecture and education center. The institute was named for {% person "ralph-waldo-emerson" %}Ralph Waldo Emerson{% endperson %}, an influential 19th-century writer and thinker who died in April 1882.</p>

<p>{% person "frank-j-mead" %}Frank Mead{% endperson %}, {% person "michael-lang" %}Michael Lang{% endperson %}, {% person "b-l-winston" %}B. L. Winston{% endperson %}, and {% person "p-o-chilstrom" %}P. O. Chilstrom{% endperson %} funded the initial land purchase. {% person "hiram-rogers-lyon" %}H. R. Lyon{% endperson %}, {% person "frank-farnsworth" %}Frank Farnsworth{% endperson %}, {% person "james-bellows" %}James Bellows{% endperson %}, {% person "warren-carpenter" %}Carpenter{% endperson %} &amp; {% person "lyman-northrop-cary" %}Cary{% endperson %}, {% person "george-h-bingenheimer" %}George Bingenheimer{% endperson %}, and {% person "e-f-doran" %}E. F. Doran{% endperson %} also helped establish the venue. Community donations raised {% inflation 3000, 1882 %} to construct the original 36-by-80-foot wooden building, completed in fall 1882, only three years after Mandan's founding.</p>

<p>The building contained “all the modern improvements, tastefully painted and finished,” with a ticket office on the right and a waiting room and women's dressing room on the opposite side. The February 18, 1882, {% place "mandan-pioneer" %}<em>Mandan Weekly Pioneer</em>{% endplace %} described the theater's place in the community: “The theater, though on rare occasions in the use of traveling professionals, is almost entirely in the hands of the {% place "mandan-dramatic-club" %}Mandan dramatic club{% endplace %}, whose members are the very cream of this city. They give frequent and most enjoyable amateur performances. The existance of such a society in so young a town as this is especially significant, as it speaks volumes as to the prosperity of the inhabitants who are so favored that they may safely unbend their minds, relax in the rigid pursuance of their avocation and let pleasure attend upon the cares of business, which makes the third feather in the cap of all Mandanites.”</p>

