  </figure>
</div>

<p>The 1884 <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> Immigrant Edition highlighted the venue as evidence of the cultural and social life of a city of 2,500, described as only three years old. The original stage at the east end was described as “large enough for all ordinary purposes,” with scenery, footlights, and the accessories needed for a “first class proscenium.” Tuttle and Wilson were the original managers; H. R. Lyon held the position by 1884.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/emerson-institute-opera-house/401-opera-house.jpg" alt="Mandan Opera House around 1906">
    <figcaption>Opera House, circa 1906</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>National theatrical guides listed the original stage as 30 by 40 feet, with a proscenium 20 feet wide and 12 feet high and seating for 300. Chairs could be rented through {% place "bingenheimer-and-taylors-drug-store" %}Bingenheimer &amp; Taylor's Drug Store{% endplace %}, and a piano and scenery were available on-site. The building was featured in a 1906 souvenir book promoting Mandan and its economic opportunities.</p>

<p>The institute hosted Fourth of July and New Year's Eve dances, live performances ranging from serious drama to vaudeville, basketball games, graduation ceremonies, and public meetings. An addition completed on the east side in spring 1893 enlarged the stage and backstage areas and raised seating capacity to 500. The expanded venue was renamed the Opera House.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/emerson-institute-opera-house/708-1906-new-years-dance-untouched.jpg" alt="New Year's Eve library-benefit dance at the Mandan Opera House">
    <figcaption>New Year's Eve library-benefit dance at the Mandan Opera House, 1903</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Dances were frequent fundraisers in the early 1900s. The {% place "ladies-of-the-library" %}Ladies of the Library{% endplace %} board held an annual New Year's Eve dance at the Opera House, often accompanied by the seven-piece {% place "lochners-orchestra" %}Lochner's Orchestra{% endplace %}. Supper was served onstage at 11 p.m. {% person "dorothy-fiske" %}Dorothy Fiske{% endperson %} served as board president.</p>

<p>The Opera House accommodated a wide range of social and cultural events from the 1880s through the 1920s, including live professional wrestling and boxing. The {% place "mandan-elks-lodge" %}Mandan Elks{% endplace %} held their first meetings there in 1911. High school athletics moved to the school gymnasium after it opened in 1919. The Opera House became less popular for live entertainment after the {% place "palace-theatre" %}Palace Theatre{% endplace %} opened in December 1916 on Third Avenue Northwest, between the {% place "lewis-and-clark-hotel" %}Lewis and Clark Hotel{% endplace %} and the {% place "hotel-donaldson" %}Hotel Donaldson{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>The building also served as Mandan's first {% place "north-dakota-national-guard" %}North Dakota National Guard{% endplace %} armory. The construction of the {% place "mandan-memorial-building" %}World War Memorial Building{% endplace %} in 1934 diminished the need for the Opera House. The memorial building also served as an armory and now houses Mandan City Hall. The Opera House was demolished and its lot sold.</p>
